# =============================================================================
# FAULTMAVEN — STANDALONE CONFIGURATION
# =============================================================================
# You only MUST set ONE thing: an LLM provider's API key (step 1 below).
# Everything else has a working default and can stay untouched.
#
#   1. cp .env.example .env
#   2. pick a provider + set its API key (section 1)
#   3. ./faultmaven.sh start        →    http://localhost:3333
#
# HOW DEFAULTS WORK
#   - Commented `# KEY=value` lines below show the DEFAULT that the app already
#     uses (from settings.py). Leave them commented to accept the default;
#     uncomment + change a line only to override it.
#   - settings.py is the source of truth for defaults; this file mirrors it, and
#     scripts/check_env_example_sync.py fails CI if the two ever drift.
#   - The JWT signing secret is auto-generated and persisted (data/.jwt_secret)
#     on first run — you do NOT need to set it.
#
# This file is the STANDALONE surface only (single process, single user: SQLite,
# in-process cache, embedded ChromaDB, local files, local auth). CLOUD settings
# (Postgres, Redis, OAuth, multi-tenancy, observability) are NOT configured here
# — they live in Kubernetes ConfigMaps/Secrets (ADR-004). FaultMaven Cloud users
# don't need this file: point the extension at https://app.faultmaven.ai
#
# Running standalone on a remote box? Keep localhost and tunnel:
#   ssh -L 3333:localhost:3333 -L 8090:localhost:8090 user@server
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. LLM PROVIDER  (REQUIRED — the one thing you must set)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED: choose a provider AND set its credential. There is NO default
# provider — if CHAT_PROVIDER is unset, or its API key (or LOCAL_LLM_URL for
# 'local') is missing, the server refuses to start. Cloud providers need a key;
# 'local' (Ollama/vLLM) needs a URL instead.
# Options: openai | anthropic | gemini | fireworks | groq | huggingface |
#          cohere | openrouter | local
# Uncomment and set to YOUR provider (the value below is only a syntax example):
# CHAT_PROVIDER=openai
#
# MODEL REQUIREMENTS (learned over time — getting these wrong is the usual cause
# of poor results; check them before pinning a non-default model for CHAT):
#
# 1) SCHEMA ENFORCEMENT. FaultMaven drives the engine with large schema-
#    constrained responses, so CHAT_PROVIDER must ENFORCE the schema natively
#    (tool calling or json_schema response_format). A provider that only
#    requests it in-prompt lets the model omit required fields → the engine
#    drops state → empty/degraded investigations.
#      Enforced (good for CHAT): openai, anthropic, gemini (1.5+, incl.
#        2.5/3.x), openrouter routed to an openai/* model, groq gpt-oss.
#      Best-effort only (fine for cheap CLASSIFIER/SYNTHESIS overrides, NOT for
#        primary CHAT): fireworks (incl. deepseek-v3/minimax), groq
#        (llama/mixtral), cohere, huggingface, local.
# 2) TOOL CALLING. The investigation agent calls tools (search_file, KB,
#    deep_analysis); a model without function/tool calling loses them and
#    degrades badly. HuggingFace's Inference API has none — don't use it for CHAT.
# 3) CONTEXT-WINDOW HEADROOM. One turn runs a multi-step tool loop whose
#    accumulated context grows well past the flat prompt budget
#    (PROMPT_TARGET_TOKENS, default 32K — section 2). The window must leave room
#    for that loop + the model's reasoning + its output, so prefer a generous
#    one (~320K+ is comfortable). The curated defaults span 200K (Claude) to 1M
#    (Gemini / GPT-4.1 / GPT-5); Claude's 200K is the tightest but still ~6× the
#    budget — fine for typical cases, reach for a 1M-window model on very deep
#    ones. The 32K budget does NOT scale with the window: the window is
#    headroom, not fill.
# 4) THINKING UNDER CONTROL. "Thinking"/reasoning models bill hidden reasoning
#    against the output budget and can starve the response into a truncated or
#    empty result. FaultMaven caps this automatically for Gemini 3.x; if you pin
#    another reasoning model (o1/o3, …) watch for empty/truncated turns.

# --- Set the API key for the provider you chose (uncomment ONE) ---
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# FIREWORKS_API_KEY=
# GROQ_API_KEY=
# HUGGINGFACE_API_KEY=
# COHERE_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# --- Local LLM (no API key; set CHAT_PROVIDER=local) ---
# LOCAL_LLM_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434   # Linux: http://172.17.0.1:11434
# LOCAL_LLM_MODEL=llama3.2
#   If you use a local model you MUST set PROMPT_TARGET_TOKENS below to fit its
#   context window. Note: Ollama defaults num_ctx to ~2048-8192 regardless of
#   the model's nominal size — so set e.g. PROMPT_TARGET_TOKENS=8000 (or raise
#   num_ctx on the Ollama side). Same applies to any provider/model whose
#   context window is below the 32000 default.

# --- Default model per provider (override only to pin a different one) ---
# Defaults favor performance (token-usage billing → quality = your experience).
# IMPORTANT: every default model shown below has a large context window (200K–1M,
# see requirement 3 above), so the default PROMPT_TARGET_TOKENS=32000 (section 2)
# fits all of them with headroom and no extra config. If you pin a DIFFERENT
# model here — or use a provider with no
# default below (local, huggingface) — you are responsible for setting
# PROMPT_TARGET_TOKENS to match THAT model's context window. Smaller model →
# lower it (e.g. 8000); larger/advanced model → you may raise it.
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5.4-mini
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-6
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash
# Fireworks is BEST-EFFORT for structured output (no native schema enforcement)
# — see requirement 1 above; prefer an enforced provider for CHAT_PROVIDER.
# FIREWORKS_MODEL=accounts/fireworks/models/deepseek-v3
# GROQ_MODEL=llama-3.3-70b-versatile
# COHERE_MODEL=command-r-plus
# OPENROUTER_MODEL=anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6
# HuggingFace works but is NOT recommended: its Inference API does not support
# tool calling, so investigation quality is degraded (no search_file, etc.).
# HUGGINGFACE_MODEL=mistralai/Mistral-Large-Instruct-2411

# Tool calling (requirement 2) is enforced at startup: if your investigation
# model (DA_PROVIDER, else CHAT_PROVIDER) can't do tool calling, the server
# REFUSES TO START — the engine needs tools to gather evidence, and concluding
# without them is unsafe. To run degraded/offline anyway (e.g. a local model
# with no tools: no search_file/deep_analysis, structural-index summaries only),
# opt in explicitly. The usual fix is to use a tool-capable CHAT_PROVIDER
# (anthropic, openai, gemini) — or set DA_PROVIDER to override just the
# investigation provider while keeping a different CHAT_PROVIDER.
# ALLOW_TOOLLESS_INVESTIGATION=false

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. OPTIONAL  (defaults shown; uncomment to change)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Prompt budget (tokens) — the whole assembled prompt is budget-allocated to
# this flat target, independent of the model's context window. 32000 is safe
# for the curated cloud models above (200K–1M context); the window is headroom
# for the tool loop + reasoning + output, not extra prompt fill. FaultMaven
# clamps this down automatically for models it knows are smaller; for models it
# does not know (local/custom), it trusts this value — so set it yourself to fit:
#   - LOCAL models: set to your model's usable context, e.g. 8000. Ollama
#     defaults num_ctx to ~2048-8192 unless you raise it, so the prompt will be
#     truncated if this is left at 32000.
#   - Advanced/large models: raise it (e.g. 64000) to use more context — note
#     this increases per-turn cost linearly.
# PROMPT_TARGET_TOKENS=32000

# Web search — enabled by default, but only works once a Tavily key is set:
# ENABLE_WEB_SEARCH=true
# TAVILY_API_KEY=

# Runbook Cause matcher (experimental, OFF by default). When on, a retrieved
# runbook whose causal chain matches the case is instantiated as a CANDIDATE
# prior + a capped hypothesis, and its documented fixes are surfaced once the
# cause is established. Boot-time flag — restart after changing. Validate via the
# sim plan (docs/architecture/investigation-engine/runbook-cause-matcher-sim-plan.md)
# before enabling in production; it adds an LLM call per matched case. Set to
# true to enable (the default below mirrors settings.py — OFF).
# ENABLE_RUNBOOK_CAUSE_MATCHER=false

# Log level: DEBUG | INFO | WARNING | ERROR
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# API port (the process runner, scripts/faultmaven-dev.sh, reads this):
# PORT=8090

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. AUTH  (standalone = single-user local auth)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Standalone always uses local auth; OAuth/multi-user is a cloud capability.
# AUTH_MODE=local
#
# The HS256 secret that signs your local session tokens is AUTO-GENERATED and
# persisted to data/.jwt_secret on first run — no action needed. To pin your own
# (e.g. to share tokens across machines), uncomment and set a long random value:
# JWT_SECRET_KEY=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. CONTAINER IMAGES  (advanced — only relevant to ./faultmaven.sh)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ./faultmaven.sh start pulls pre-built images from GHCR. Tags default to
# :latest (auto-refreshed each start); pin for a reproducible, version-matched
# stack (a release version or an immutable sha-<short-sha>):
# FM_IMAGE_TAG=latest
# FM_DASHBOARD_IMAGE_TAG=latest
#
# To build from your own source instead: ./faultmaven.sh start --build (API) /
# --build-dashboard (Dashboard).

# =============================================================================
# Notes
# =============================================================================
# • Fixed standalone backends (not set here): SQLite, in-process cache, embedded
#   ChromaDB, local file storage, local auth. Postgres/Redis/S3/multi-tenancy are
#   cloud-only and configured via Kubernetes, never in this file.
# • DEPLOYMENT_MODE defaults to 'standalone' — do NOT set 'cloud' here. Cloud is
#   driven by Kubernetes config, and the server fails fast on boot if it is told
#   'cloud' without the required cloud infrastructure (ADR-004 coherence gate).
